\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces BDNK characteristic speeds vs.\ temperature for (a) the quark-gluon plasma and (b) neutron star matter. The sound speed $c_s/c$ (blue/pink) and the maximum BDNK dissipative characteristic speed (green) are both bounded by $c$ (gray dotted line). The Eckart/Landau theory predicts acausal infinite-speed dissipative propagation (orange dashed). The QCD crossover at $T_c \approx 155$\,MeV and the deconfinement transition region are indicated.}}{35}{figure.1.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Alfv\'en speed $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$ (colored solid) and fast magnetosonic speed $v_{\mathrm {f}}/c$ (green dash-dotted) versus magnetic field strength $B$ for (a) neutron star, (b) magnetar, and (c) relativistic jet environments. The classical Alfv\'en speed (gray dashed) can exceed $c$; the relativistic expression automatically saturates below $c$ (gray dotted). Sound speed $c_s/c$ is shown for reference (blue dashed).}}{49}{figure.2.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Squared sound speed $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2/c^2$ as a function of energy density (in units of nuclear saturation energy density $\varepsilon _{\mathrm {nuc}} \approx 150$\,MeV/fm$^3$) for three representative equations of state: ideal relativistic gas (blue), quark-gluon plasma (brown), and nuclear matter (pink). The causality bound $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2 = c^2$ (red line) and the conformal limit $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2/c^2 = 1/3$ (gray dotted) are shown. The QCD crossover produces a pronounced dip in the QGP sound speed near $\varepsilon \approx \varepsilon _{\mathrm {nuc}}$.}}{61}{figure.2.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The relativistic compactness parameter $\xi =p_{0}/(\varepsilon _{0}c^{2})$ across astrophysical regimes. Relativistic corrections to the critical Rayleigh number exceed 1\% when $\xi >0.01$ (dashed red line) and become of order unity when $\xi \sim 1/3$ (dot-dashed line).}}{71}{figure.3.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces (a)~Critical Rayleigh number $R_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}$ vs.\ $\xi $ for three boundary types. (b)~$\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ contours in NS ocean parameter space ($\xi =0.015$).}}{85}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces (a)~Classical growth rate vs.\ wavenumber at several $\mathrm {Ra}/\mathrm {Ra}_c$. (b)~BDNK (3 modes) vs.\ IS (5 modes): dashed curves show IS relaxation modes absent in BDNK.}}{95}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces (a)~Rayleigh quotient ratio (green) and critical Ra ratio (red) vs.\ $\xi $. (b)~Energy balance at marginal stability: viscous dissipation, buoyancy release, and entropy production.}}{102}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces (a)~Critical Rayleigh numbers for 4 regimes and 3 boundary types. (b)~Marginal stability curves $R_{c}(a)$ showing upward shift with $\xi $.}}{108}{figure.4.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces (a)~Pattern selection phase diagram: rolls stable at low $gd/c^{2}$ and $c_s^{2}/c^{2}$; hexagons preferred at larger values. (b)~Landau coefficients vs.\ $\xi $ at $gd/c^{2}=0.01$.}}{114}{figure.4.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces (a)~Astrophysical parameter space in the ($\xi $, Ra) plane. Shaded rectangles show estimated ranges for various systems. Black dashed lines mark the critical Rayleigh number (both-free and both-rigid boundaries) as a function of $\xi $. The blue-shaded region below the curves is stable. (b)~Observable signatures of relativistic convection: convective heat flux suppression and velocity reduction as functions of $\xi $ (at fixed supercriticality $R/R_c=2$).}}{121}{figure.4.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Frame-dragging effect on inertial waves in rotating neutron stars. \textbf {(a)}~Inertial-wave frequency $|\sigma | = 2\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}\cos \vartheta $ vs.\ wavevector angle~$\vartheta $ for a millisecond pulsar ($\Omega = 4498$~rad\,s$^{-1}$) at several compactness values. Frame dragging reduces $\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}$ and hence the wave frequency. \textbf {(b)}~Percentage shifts in frequency (frame-dragging) and damping rate (enthalpy correction) as functions of stellar compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. For a canonical neutron star with $\mathcal {C} = 0.2$, the corrections are of order 5--30\%.}}{134}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for rotating convection in millisecond pulsars. The critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ is plotted against the physical Taylor number $\mathrm {Ta}$ for several values of the enthalpy ratio $w/(\rho _0c^{2})$. The shaded band marks the regime $\mathrm {Ta} \sim 10^{12}\text {--}10^{18}$ relevant to millisecond pulsars. Solid curves: stationary convection; thin curves: overstability ($\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-3}$). The relativistic enhancement of $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ shifts the stability boundary upward, strengthening rotational stabilisation. The top axis shows the corresponding rotation rate for $\nu = 10$~cm$^{2}$\,s$^{-1}$ and $d = 100$~m.}}{146}{figure.5.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Overstability in neutron star cores. \textbf {(a)}~Growth rate of the overstable mode near onset for classical (Navier--Stokes), BDNK first-order, and Israel--Stewart formulations, at $\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-3}$, $T_{1} = 10^{4}$. The BDNK curve is shifted downward by the enthalpy factor $\rho _0c^{2}/w$; the IS curve includes a small correction from $\tau _{q}\omega \neq 0$. \textbf {(b)}~Oscillation frequency of the overstable mode vs.\ relativistic Taylor number for several enthalpy ratios. The frequency follows $\omega \propto \mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}^{1/2}$ (inertial scaling) but is suppressed by $(\rho _0c^{2}/w)^{1/2}$ in the relativistic theory.}}{155}{figure.5.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Double-diffusive convection in proto-neutron stars. \textbf {(a)}~Stability regions in the (Lewis number, density ratio) plane for nuclear matter with $\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-2}$. The shaded band marks the proto-NS neutrino regime ($\mathrm {Le} \sim 10$--100). The arrow indicates the upward shift of the oscillatory boundary due to relativistic enthalpy corrections. \textbf {(b)}~Critical Rayleigh number vs.\ Taylor number for several values of the density ratio $R_{\rho }$, showing how the stabilising lepton gradient raises the convective threshold. The proto-NS regime is highlighted.}}{168}{figure.5.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Alfv\'en speed $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$ as a function of magnetic field strength $B$ for four representative neutron star densities. Solid lines: relativistic formula~\eqref {eq:rel4-24}; dashed lines: classical formula $V_A = B/\sqrt {4\pi \rho }$. The horizontal dotted line marks the causal limit $v_{\mathrm {A}}= c$. The relativistic saturation becomes significant for $B \gtrsim 10^{15}$~G in the outer core and for $B \gtrsim 10^{16}$~G in the inner core.}}{181}{figure.6.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for magneto-convection: critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus Chandrasekhar number $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$. The classical curve (solid blue) is compared with relativistic curves at $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2} = 0.01$, $0.05$, and $0.1$ representative of neutron star outer core, core, and magnetar interior respectively. The shaded region marks the magnetar parameter regime. The $\pi ^{2}Q$ asymptote (dotted grey) is approached for all boundary conditions at large $Q$.}}{192}{figure.6.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Numerical verification of the $\pi ^{2}Q$ law. \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus Chandrasekhar number $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ for free boundaries (solid blue), rigid boundaries (dashed purple), and relativistic free boundaries with $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2} = 0.05$ (dash-dotted red). \emph {Right:} The ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}/Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ converges to $\pi ^{2}$ (dotted grey line) for all boundary conditions as $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}\to \infty $, confirming the universality of the $\pi ^{2}Q$ law in the relativistic theory.}}{201}{figure.6.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Oblique magnetic field and thermal convection in magnetar interiors. \emph {Left:} Effective Chandrasekhar number (normalised to the aligned-field classical value) versus the angle $\vartheta $ between $\mathbf {B}$ and $\mathbf {g}$, for several values of $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$. \emph {Right:} Overstable frequency shift $\sigma _{\mathrm {rel}}/\sigma _{\mathrm {class}}$ versus $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$ at $\vartheta = 45^{\circ }$.}}{210}{figure.6.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Magneto-inertial wave dispersion in rotating neutron stars. \emph {Left:} Frequency $\omega $ versus wavenumber $k$ for the two transverse magneto-inertial branches at four values of $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$. The light line $\omega = kc$ (dotted grey) is never crossed, confirming causality. \emph {Right:} Phase velocity $v_{\mathrm {ph}}/c$ versus $k$, showing that all modes remain sub-luminal.}}{218}{figure.7.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional stability surface for rotating magnetar convection: $\log _{10}\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}_{c}$ as a function of $\log _{10}\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ and $\log _{10}Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ (two free boundaries). Warmer colours indicate higher critical Rayleigh numbers. The competition between rotation and magnetic field is visible as a valley along the $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ axis at moderate $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$.}}{227}{figure.8.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Magnitude of relativistic corrections across astrophysical systems. \emph {Top:} Individual correction parameters: magnetic ($v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$, red), rotational ($\Omega ^{2}d^{2}/c^{2}$, blue), and thermal ($p/(\rho c^{2})$, green). \emph {Bottom:} Leading-order fractional correction to the critical Rayleigh number. Blue bars: negligible ($<10^{-6}$); orange: small ($10^{-6}$--$10^{-2}$); red: significant ($>10^{-2}$). Only in neutron star cores and magnetar interiors do the relativistic corrections reach the percent level.}}{235}{figure.8.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces TOV equilibrium profiles for the Schwarzschild interior (uniform energy density) solution at four compactness values $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. \emph {Left:} Energy density (constant) and enthalpy density $(\varepsilon +p)/\varepsilon _{c}$. \emph {Centre:} Pressure profile $p(r)/\varepsilon _{c}$. \emph {Right:} Metric potential $\Phi (r)/c^{2}$. The steepening of $p(r)$ and deepening of the potential well with increasing compactness control the relativistic corrections to convective stability (cf.\ \S \,\ref {subsec:rel-56-summary}).}}{244}{figure.8.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {rel}}$ vs.\ compactness for $l=1$, 2, 3 modes (free boundary, uniform-density sphere). Dotted lines mark the Newtonian values. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {rel}} / \mathrm {Ra}_{c}^{(N)}$. The shaded band marks the compactness range of observed neutron stars ($0.1$--$0.25$). Relativistic corrections reduce the critical Rayleigh number by 30--50\% for typical NS compactness, owing to the enhanced effective gravity and pressure--inertia coupling.}}{255}{figure.9.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ for the $l=1$ mode vs.\ compactness $GM/(Rc^{2})$ for three representative nuclear EOSs. Filled circles mark the compactness of a $1.4\,M_{\odot }$ neutron star for each EOS. The horizontal dotted line is the Newtonian value. Softer EOSs (BSk21) produce higher compactness and hence lower critical Rayleigh numbers, implying easier onset of core convection.}}{263}{figure.9.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh number for convection in a spherical shell vs.\ shell thickness ratio $\eta $ for various compactness parameters $\xi $. The Newtonian limit ($\xi = 0$) is shown as a dotted gray line. The shaded region marks the range $\eta \approx 0.85$--$0.95$ typical of neutron star crusts. Relativistic corrections increase $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ by factors of 2--3 at typical NS compactness ($\xi \simeq 0.3$--$0.4$), substantially inhibiting convective overturn.}}{274}{figure.9.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Ratio $\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}/\Omega $ (effective rotation including frame-dragging) at three radial positions within a $1.4\,M_{\odot }$, $R = 10$~km neutron star. Frame-dragging reduces the effective rotation by 15--40\%, with the strongest effect in the deep interior. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}/\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {Newton}}$ (volume-averaged) for several compactness values. The vertical dotted line marks PSR~J1748$-$2446ad, the fastest known pulsar at 716~Hz~(Hessels et al.\ 2006). The Lense--Thirring effect reduces the rotational stabilisation by up to 30\%, lowering the threshold for convective onset.}}{287}{figure.9.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces Left: relativistic specific angular momentum $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}$ versus $r/r_{g}$ for Keplerian geodesic flow in Schwarzschild ($a/M=0$) and Kerr ($a/M=0.5,\,0.998$) spacetimes. Filled markers indicate the ISCO for each case. Right: radial derivative $d(\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2})/dr$; the Rayleigh-stable region ($d\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}/dr>0$) is shaded. The ISCO coincides with $d\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}/dr=0$ in every case.}}{295}{figure.10.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Rayleigh discriminant $\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for Keplerian circular orbits in Schwarzschild and Kerr spacetimes. The discriminant vanishes at the ISCO (filled circles) and is positive outside. Higher black-hole spin shifts the ISCO inward, extending the stable Keplerian flow region. The shaded area marks the Rayleigh-unstable regime ($\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}<0$).}}{301}{figure.10.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.3}{\ignorespaces Epicyclic and orbital frequencies versus $r/r_{g}$ for Kerr black holes with $a/M=0$ (Schwarzschild), $0.5$, and $0.998$. Frequencies are shown in Hz for $M=10\,M_{\odot }$. The inverted triangles mark the maximum of $\kappa _{r}$, which sets the highest possible radial QPO frequency. The vertical grey lines indicate the ISCO for each spin.}}{309}{figure.10.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.4}{\ignorespaces Left: ratio of relativistic to classical Reynolds number versus the correction parameter~$\xi $, with astrophysical regimes indicated. Right: relativistic enhancement of the critical Taylor number (solid) and suppression of the Shakura--Sunyaev $\alpha $-parameter (dashed) as functions of~$\xi $.}}{316}{figure.10.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.5}{\ignorespaces Left: critical Taylor number $T_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus $\mu =\Omega _{2}/\Omega _{1}$ in the narrow-gap limit for several values of the relativistic correction parameter~$\xi $. Right: ratio $T_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}/T_{c}^{\mathrm {cl}}=(1+\xi )^{2}$ as a function of~$\xi $, with astrophysical regimes marked.}}{323}{figure.10.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.6}{\ignorespaces Left: critical Taylor number versus stability parameter $\kappa $ for the wide-gap case $\eta =0.5$, comparing classical and relativistic predictions for several values of $p/\rho _{0}c^{2}$. Right: relativistic correction $\delta _{\mathrm {rel}}$ to the critical Taylor number as a function of gap ratio~$\eta $. The thick-disk regime ($\eta \lesssim 0.5$, wide gap) shows slightly larger corrections than the thin-disk regime ($\eta \to 1$, narrow gap).}}{329}{figure.10.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.7}{\ignorespaces Dean number vs.\ disk curvature for accretion stream curved channels. Left: critical Reynolds number as a function of $d/R_1$ for several values of $V_m/c$. Right: relativistic Dean parameter $\Lambda _{\mathrm {rel},c}$ vs.\ $V_m/c$. Shaded bands indicate astrophysically relevant regimes.}}{335}{figure.10.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.8}{\ignorespaces Stability map for a disk with radial pressure gradient: critical Taylor number vs.\ $\lambda $ for several values of $\Xi $. The relativistic correction $(1+\Xi )^2$ uniformly raises the stability boundary.}}{342}{figure.10.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.9}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for astrophysical jets with axial flow plus rotation. Left: $\Psi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for pure axial flow. Right: $\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for combined rotation and axial flow. Negative values indicate instability.}}{350}{figure.10.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.10}{\ignorespaces Viscous spiral flow in accretion: Re--Ta parameter space. Left: stability boundaries for several BDNK parameters $\mathcal {T}$. Right: critical Taylor number vs.\ $\mathcal {T}$ for fixed Re. The shaded band indicates relativistic accretion column conditions.}}{357}{figure.10.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.11}{\ignorespaces MRI dispersion in a Keplerian disk: growth rate $\sigma /\Omega $ vs.\ wavenumber for several $v_A/c$. Left: classical. Right: relativistic (bounded $v_A$).}}{368}{figure.10.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.12}{\ignorespaces Kink modes in NS magnetospheres. Left: growth rate vs.\ wavenumber for several $\Xi $. Right: critical axial field for stabilisation in the $(\Xi , v_A/c)$ plane.}}{376}{figure.10.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.13}{\ignorespaces Dissipative MRI: critical $\mathscr {T}_c(Q)$ for conducting and non-conducting boundaries. Left: co-rotating ($\mu > 0$). Right: counter-rotating ($\mu = -1$). Solid: classical; dashed: relativistic ($v_A/c = 0.1$).}}{387}{figure.10.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.14}{\ignorespaces GRMHD MRI near the ISCO. Left: maximum growth rate vs.\ radius for Kerr black holes with various spin parameters; dotted lines mark the ISCO. Right: effective $\alpha $-parameter from MRI-driven turbulence compared with GRMHD simulation data.}}{392}{figure.10.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces Shock-driven Rayleigh--Taylor instability in core-collapse supernovae. \textit {Left:} Growth rate $\sigma $ as a function of the density ratio $\rho _{2}/\rho _{1}$ across the composition interface, for classical ($\xi =0$) and several relativistic values of $\xi = p/(\rho c^{2})$. \textit {Right:} Classical vs.\ relativistic Atwood number. Parameters correspond to a proto-neutron star of mass $1.4\,M_{\odot }$ and shock radius $150\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {km}$.}}{402}{figure.11.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces Variational bounds on the RT growth rate for an exponentially stratified relativistic fluid. \textit {Left:} Exact growth rates for modes $m = 1, 2, 3$ (solid) compared with the upper bound from a parabolic trial function (dotted). The asymptotic limit $n_{\max } = \sqrt {g\beta }$ is shown as a dashed line. \textit {Right:} Maximum growth rate ($m=1$) for different values of the relativistic parameter~$\xi $, showing the systematic reduction of the bound due to enhanced inertia.}}{409}{figure.11.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.3}{\ignorespaces Viscous RT instability at the contact interface in binary neutron star mergers. \textit {Left:} Normalised growth rate $\sigma /\sqrt {g/L}$ vs.\ effective Reynolds number for several relativistic Atwood numbers. \textit {Right:} Damping timescale ratio $\tau _{\mathrm {damp}}/\tau _{\mathrm {inviscid}}$ comparing the BDNK first-order theory (solid) with the Israel--Stewart second-order theory (dashed, $\tau _{\pi }/\tau _{0} = 0.1$).}}{415}{figure.11.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces Centrifugal stabilisation of RT instability in a relativistic accretion disk. \textit {Left:} Growth rate $n$ vs.\ wavenumber $k$ for increasing angular velocity $\Omega _{\mathrm {rel}}$. \textit {Right:} Ratio $n(\Omega )/n(0)$ as a function of co-rotation speed $\Omega R/c$ for several wavenumbers, comparing Newtonian (dashed) and relativistic (solid) predictions. The relativistic Coriolis enhancement $\Gamma _{\Omega }^{2}$ provides significantly stronger stabilisation at high rotation rates.}}{425}{figure.12.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.1}{\ignorespaces Magnetic stabilisation of RT instability in relativistic jets. \textit {Left:} Critical magnetic field strength for complete stabilisation vs.\ enthalpy ratio, for different Alfv\'en speeds. The relativistic correction factor $\sqrt {1 - v_{A}^{2}/c^{2}}$ reduces the threshold. \textit {Right:} Growth rate vs.\ wavenumber for a vertical magnetic field with $v_{A}/c = 0.5$. The growth rate saturates at $k \to \infty $ (dotted lines), unlike the unbounded $\sqrt {gk}$ behaviour of the unmagnetised case (dashed).}}{435}{figure.13.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.2}{\ignorespaces Neutron star oscillation modes. \textit {Left:} Relativistic correction factor $\sigma _{\mathrm {rel}}/\sigma _{\mathrm {Newt}}$ for Kelvin modes $\ell = 2$--$5$ as a function of compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. The shaded band indicates the typical NS compactness range. \textit {Right:} Physical $f$-mode and $g$-mode frequencies for $\ell = 2$ as a function of NS mass, comparing relativistic (solid) and Newtonian (dashed) predictions.}}{444}{figure.13.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical KH velocity (normalised to $\sqrt {g/k}$) vs.\ jet Lorentz factor for density ratios $\eta = \hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {amb}}/\hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {jet}}$ ranging from 0.01 (light jet) to 1.0 (matched). \textbf {Right:} Lab-frame growth rate suppression $\sigma _{\mathrm {lab}}/\sigma _0 \propto \Gamma ^{-2}$, with positions of M87, 3C\,273, and 3C\,279 marked. }}{452}{figure.14.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic Richardson number $J_{\mathrm {rel}}(z)$ across a $\tanh $ shear layer for $\Gamma _0 = 1$ (classical), 2, 5, and 10. The dashed line marks the Miles--Howard threshold $J_{\mathrm {rel}} = 1/4$. \textbf {Right:} Critical shear-layer width (normalised) vs.\ $\Gamma $ for stability, with observed positions of M87 and Cen~A marked. }}{462}{figure.15.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Lab-frame KH growth rate (normalised to $k\,c$) vs.\ Lorentz factor for hot ($c_{s}=c/\sqrt {3}$) and cold ($c_{s}=0.1\,c$) jets, including Gaussian structured profiles with $\Gamma _{\mathrm {core}} = 100$ and 300. The shaded band marks the GRB regime ($\Gamma \sim 100$--$1000$). \textbf {Right:} Stability map in the $(\Gamma ,\,c_{s}/c)$ plane; the colour scale shows $\log _{10}(\sigma _{\mathrm {lab}}/kc)$, with dashed lines marking the compressible stabilisation boundary for $c_{s}=c/\sqrt {3}$ and $c_{s}=0.1\,c$. }}{471}{figure.16.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical magnetisation $\sigma _{\mathrm {crit}}$ for complete KH stabilisation vs.\ jet Lorentz factor, for density ratios $\eta = 0.01$--$1.0$. \textbf {Right:} Normalised KH growth rate vs.\ magnetisation parameter $\sigma $ for $\Gamma = 3$, 10, and 30 (with $\eta = 0.1$). The vertical dashed lines mark the critical $\sigma $ for each $\Gamma $. }}{479}{figure.16.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic dispersion relation for the varicose mode of an infinite self-gravitating cylinder at several values of the compactness $\mathcal {C}$. The critical wavenumber $x_{a}=1.0668$ is unchanged, but the peak shifts to smaller $x$ (longer wavelengths) as $\mathcal {C}$ increases. \textbf {Right:} Ratio of the relativistic to Newtonian most-unstable fragmentation wavelength as a function of $\mathcal {C}$, with astrophysical regimes indicated.}}{486}{figure.17.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Dispersion relation for the gravitational instability of a magnetized relativistic cylinder at different axial field strengths $H/H_{0,\mathrm {rel}}$. The magnetic field raises the critical wavenumber (shorter minimum unstable wavelength) and reduces the peak growth rate. \textbf {Right:} Minimum unstable wavelength (solid, left axis) and maximum growth rate (dashed, right axis) as functions of $H/H_{0,\mathrm {rel}}$, with astrophysical jet regimes indicated.}}{500}{figure.17.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic suppression of the capillary growth rate and enhancement of the breakup timescale as functions of the enthalpy ratio $w/\rho = (\varepsilon +p)/(\rho c^{2})$, with astrophysical regimes indicated. \textbf {Right:} Observer-frame breakup timescale for a GRB jet (cocoon-confined, $R=10^{7}\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {cm}$) as a function of bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma $, for several internal enthalpy ratios. The grey band marks the observed GRB variability range.}}{512}{figure.17.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic Kruskal--Shafranov critical ratio $B_{z}/B_{\varphi }$ for the $m=-1$ kink mode as a function of bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma $, for several axial wavenumbers $kR_{1}$. Dotted lines show the classical (static) values. Relativistic bulk flow strongly suppresses the required stabilising field. \textbf {Right:} Application to magnetar giant flares: the dipolar $B_{z}/B_{\varphi }$ ratio (solid) decreases with distance from the neutron star, crossing the kink-stability threshold (dashed horizontal lines) at radii that depend on the fireball Lorentz factor $\gamma $.}}{523}{figure.17.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.5}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical adiabatic index $\gamma _{c}$ versus stellar compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$ for three structure-dependent values of $\kappa $ (eq.~\ref {eq:rel-13-gamma-c}). The shaded bands mark the compactness ranges of white dwarfs ($\mathcal {C} \sim 10^{-4}$--$10^{-3}$) and neutron stars ($\mathcal {C} \sim 0.1$--$0.35$). Supermassive stars (SMS) with radiation-dominated interiors ($\gamma _{\mathrm {eff}} \approx 4/3$) are driven unstable by even a tiny GR correction. \textbf {Right:} Normalised oscillation frequency $\omega ^{2}$ versus $\gamma $ for several compactness values, showing how the stability boundary shifts rightward as $\mathcal {C}$ increases. Configurations in the red-shaded region are dynamically unstable. }}{532}{figure.17.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.6}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Ratio of relativistic to classical Jeans mass as a function of the equation-of-state parameter $w = p/\varepsilon $. The relativistic Jeans mass is always \emph {smaller} than the classical value, reflecting the pressure-destabilisation effect. The radiation-dominated case ($w = 1/3$) and the causal limit ($w = 1$) are marked. \textbf {Right:} Enhancement of the Jeans wavenumber $k_{J,\mathrm {rel}}/k_{J,\mathrm {classical}}$, showing that GR renders shorter-wavelength modes gravitationally unstable. The shaded blue region indicates the additional $k$-space made unstable by relativistic effects. }}{539}{figure.17.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.7}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Toomre $Q$ parameter as a function of galactocentric radius for a model spiral galaxy with a flat rotation curve ($V_{c} = 220$~km/s) and exponential surface density profile. The thermal-only case ($c_{s} = 10$~km/s) is compared with the magnetically supported case ($B = 5\,\mu $G). Orange bands mark schematic spiral-arm locations where enhanced surface density drives $Q$ down toward the instability threshold $Q = 1$. \textbf {Right:} Normalised relativistic $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ as a function of $c_{s}/c$ for different magnetic field strengths in a compact disk, showing how relativistic effects (enthalpy corrections, bounded $v_{A}$) modify the stability boundary at high sound speeds. }}{545}{figure.17.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.1}{\ignorespaces Stability map in the $(\beta , q)$ plane from the MHD energy principle $\delta ^{2}W$. Red/yellow regions are unstable ($\delta ^{2}W < 0$); green/blue regions are stable ($\delta ^{2}W > 0$). The solid black curve marks the marginal stability boundary $\delta ^{2}W = 0$. \textbf {Left:} Classical (Newtonian) result. The $q = 1$ boundary (white dashed) corresponds to the $m = 1$ kink instability; the diagonal boundary reflects the ballooning $\beta $-limit. \textbf {Right:} Relativistic case with $v_{A}/c = 0.3$ (representative of neutron-star magnetospheres or hot accretion flows). The dashed white curve reproduces the classical boundary for comparison. Relativistic corrections---enhanced enthalpy inertia and bounded Alfv\'en speed---shrink the stable region, lowering the critical $\beta $-limit. }}{556}{figure.18.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Normalised Parker instability growth rate $\sigma ^{2}/(g/H)$ as a function of normalised horizontal wavenumber $k_{x}H$ for several values of the plasma $\beta = 8\pi p/B^{2}$. Solid curves are the classical result; dashed curves show the relativistic case with $c_{s}/c = 0.3$. Relativistic effects enhance the peak growth rate through the factor $(1 + 3c_{s}^{2}/c^{2})/2$ in the gravitational source. \textbf {Right:} Ratio of relativistic to classical critical wavelength (blue, left axis) and peak growth rate (red, right axis) as functions of $c_{s}/c$ for $\beta = 1$. The critical wavelength \emph {decreases} (shorter wavelengths become unstable) while the growth rate \emph {increases} with increasing relativistic parameter. }}{562}{figure.18.2}%
